EverParakeet by EverPaper (iPhone demo)
=======================================

Demo code: Copyright 2026 Everpaper, CC-BY-4.0 (see LICENSE).

Packages/EverParakeetKit/
    The EverParakeet Swift runtime, version 1.1.1, copied unchanged from the swift/ folder of the Hugging Face
    repository EverPaper/everparakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3-coreai at revision 3273ae066c0d7f46940de4917fb731fdb89558b7. Every
    file matches the CHECKSUMS.sha256 of that revision. It is CC-BY-4.0, except three files adapted from Apple's
    Core AI speech sample code (coreai-models repository), which are under Apple's BSD 3-clause licence with the
    header "Portions Copyright 2026 Apple Inc. Modifications Copyright 2026 Everpaper.":
        Sources/EverParakeetKit/MelFrontEnd.swift
        Sources/EverParakeetCoreAI/CoreAIEncoder.swift
        Sources/EverParakeetKit/AudioLoader.swift
    The full Apple licence text is in Packages/EverParakeetKit/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES. Keep that file, and the headers
    in those three files, if you copy the package.

Model weights (downloaded, not included)
    scripts/fetch-model.sh downloads EverParakeet from Hugging Face. The weights are a derivative of
    NVIDIA Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3), Copyright NVIDIA Corporation,
    CC-BY-4.0. All credit for the architecture, the tokenizer and the multilingual training goes to NVIDIA. This is
    not an official NVIDIA distribution and NVIDIA does not endorse it. The list of modifications is in the NOTICE
    file of the model repository.

Samples/
    english.wav, italian.wav, italian_then_english.wav and long_italian.wav are made from recordings in FLEURS
    (Google), https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/fleurs, licensed CC-BY-4.0: the test split, en_us and it_it
    (utterance ids are listed in README.md). The recordings are used as released (16 kHz, mono, 16-bit); the
    clips italian_then_english.wav and long_italian.wav join several of them with 0.4 s of silence between pieces;
    nothing else was changed. Conneau et al., "FLEURS: Few-shot
    Learning Evaluation of Universal Representations of Speech", 2022. The clips are shared here under the same
    CC-BY-4.0 licence. The speakers are anonymous volunteers and no personal data is included.

Fonts
    None are bundled. The app uses the system fonts (SF, New York, SF Mono).
